-- An EVENT has no JSON form, so an @export cannot take one as an input: the
-- service's input decoder reads an object only into a declared record, and the
-- builtin EVENT type has no declaration. Each rule below is refused with the
-- same error, naming the input; the rules are exported on purpose, because the
-- same rule without @export checks (see ok/event-type-resolves.l4).
--
-- The input may be an EVENT, hold one in a list or a MAYBE, reach one through a
-- synonym, or hold one in a field of a record.
DECLARE Person IS ONE OF Seller, Buyer
DECLARE Action IS ONE OF delivery, payment

DECLARE Occurrence IS AN EVENT Person Action

DECLARE Report HAS reported IS AN EVENT Person Action
                   note IS A STRING

@export The time of an event
GIVEN `the reported event` IS AN EVENT Person Action
GIVETH A NUMBER
`when it happened` `the reported event` MEANS
  CONSIDER `the reported event`
  WHEN EVENT party action time THEN time

@export Events in a list
GIVEN events IS A LIST OF EVENT Person Action
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`any events` events MEANS TRUE

@export An event that may be missing
GIVEN `maybe an event` IS A MAYBE (EVENT Person Action)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`has an event` `maybe an event` MEANS TRUE

@export An event behind a synonym
GIVEN o IS AN Occurrence
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`is an occurrence` o MEANS TRUE

@export A record with an event in a field
GIVEN r IS A Report
GIVETH A STRING
`report note` r MEANS r's note

-- Not exported: an EVENT input is fine here, and is not reported.
GIVEN e IS AN EVENT Person Action
GIVETH A NUMBER
`internal time` e MEANS
  CONSIDER e
  WHEN EVENT party action time THEN time

-- Exported with the parts of the event as inputs, and the event built inside:
-- accepted.
@export The time of the event built from its parts
GIVEN who IS A Person
      what IS AN Action
      `at time` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`time of parts` who what `at time` MEANS
  CONSIDER EVENT who what `at time`
  WHEN EVENT party action time THEN time
